== A Wife’s Active Obedience to Her Husband

=== §. 43. #emph[Of a wife’s active obedience.]

It is a good proof and test of a wife’s obedience to abstain from doing such things as she would otherwise do, if her husband’s contrary will did not restrain her. But that is not enough: an active obedience must be yielded as well as a passive one. That old law mentioned before (“your desire will be subject to your husband, and he will rule over you”) implies as much too. If she refuses to do what he wants her to do, her desire is not subject to him but to herself, nor does he rule over her.

This active part of her obedience concerns

1\. His #emph[commands:] readily doing what he lawfully #footnote[A wife’s active obedience concerns first her husband’s command.] commands.

2\. His #emph[reproofs:] carefully setting right what he justly blames.

As for the first, a wife ought to be so far from scorning to be commanded by her husband that the very knowledge she has, by any means, of her husband’s mind and will ought to have the force of a strict command with her. This readiness to obey is commended in the wives of Jacob. When Jacob had told them what reasons he had to leave their father’s house, and so intimated that he meant to leave and wanted them to go with him, then, even before he had expressed his will in particular, they readily answered, “Do whatever God #footnote[Gen 31:16.] has told you.” By this they gave him to understand that they were ready to yield to whatever he wanted done.

=== §. 44. #emph[Of a wife’s willingness to live where her husband wishes.]#footnote(numbering: (..) => "*")[Treatise 4, §. 18.]

To make this part of a wife’s obedience somewhat clearer, I will illustrate it by two or three particular instances recorded and approved in God’s word.

The first is that #emph[a wife ought to be willing to live where her husband wants her to live.]

The wives of Abraham, Isaac and Jacob showed their wifely obedience in this. Though their husbands brought them from their own country and from their fathers’ houses, yet they did not refuse to go with them, but lived in a strange country, and that in tents.

Note in particular what Jacob’s wives say to their husband in this case: “Do we have any portion or inheritance left in our father’s #footnote[Gen 31:14.] house?” By this they imply that, since it was their husband’s pleasure to go, they would no longer stay in their father’s house to look for any further portion or inheritance there.

These examples further show that if a husband has just occasion to move from one country to another, and within those countries from place to place, his wife ought to yield to go with him, if he requires it of her. Note what the apostle says: “Have we no power to take along a wife?”#footnote[1 Cor 9:5.] That question implies a strong assertion. Since the husband, then, has the power to take a wife along from place to place, she ought to submit herself to that power. This clause (“as do the other apostles and the Lord’s brothers and Cephas”) shows that this was not only a power that might be used, but one that was used by husbands and yielded to by wives.

#emph[Objection.] The examples just named are extraordinary, and on extraordinary occasions.

#emph[Answer.] Yet they may be patterns for ordinary occasions that are lawful and warrantable. Was it not an extraordinary act of Elijah to pray first that there might be no rain,#footnote[Jas 5:17.] and then again that there might be rain? Yet this is set forth as a general pattern to move us to pray for lawful things.

Now in laying down this duty I added the clause and caution of #emph[just occasion,] to answer both those who, out of discontent or superstition, leave the land where the true gospel is upheld and preached and go into idolatrous places, and also those wandering, giddy heads who, only to satisfy their own whims and to see the fashions, as we say, can never rest in one place, but are continually moving from country to country and from place to place. In such cases I think (to use the words of the apostle) a wife “is not bound.” #footnote[1 Cor 7:15.]

But if a man is sent on an embassy by his prince or country; or if a preacher or professor is called into another country, as Bucer and Peter Martyr were into England in King Edward’s days (which is usual in other countries to this day); or if a man is sentenced to long imprisonment, and on these and other such occasions he requires his wife to be with him, she ought in duty to yield to his demand.

Contrary to this is the mind and practice of many wives who, #footnote[Contrary: when a wife makes her husband live where she wants.] being attached and devoted to one place more than another, such as the place where they were born and brought up, where their greatest, best and most friends live, and where they have good acquaintance, refuse to go and live where their husband’s calling lies, however much he requires and desires it. So many husbands, to their great loss, are forced for peace’s sake to yield to their wives, and so either to give up their calling or to keep two houses. From this it follows that sometimes they must neglect their servants and calling, and sometimes be absent from their wives, if not from their children also. Some wives claim that they cannot endure the smoke of the city, others that they cannot endure the air of the country; whereas in truth their own whims and fancies stifle them more than either city smoke or country air. I cannot call such the daughters of Sarah in this. #footnote[1 Pet 3:5, 6.] They are not like those “holy women” named before, who “put their hope in God and were subject to their husbands,” but rather like that loose wife of the Levite, who would not live in her husband’s #footnote[Judg 19:2.] house in the hill country of Ephraim, but at her father’s house in Bethlehem in Judah. Such wives as I speak of may be more chaste in marriage, but in wifely subjection they are little more dutiful. Let this be taken for a fault, and it will be amended all the sooner.

=== §. 45. #emph[Of a wife’s readiness to come to her husband when he requires it.]#footnote(numbering: (..) => "*")[Treatise 4, §. 29.]

Another particular instance of a wife’s readiness to yield to her husband’s command is #emph[to come to her husband when her husband requires it,] either by calling her or by sending for her. The #footnote[Gen 31:4.] wives of Jacob named before, being sent for to come to their husband in the field where he was, made no excuse, but came at once. Wives ought to subject their wills to their husbands so far in this that, though it may seem to them somewhat beneath them to come, yet if their husbands want it so, they must yield; otherwise they seem even to “despise their husbands.”#footnote[Esth 1:17.]

Contrary to this is Vashti-like defiance, when wives think and #footnote[Contrary: defiance in refusing to come.] say that it is a servant’s part to come when called or sent for, and that they will never yield to be their husbands’ servants, to come at their command. By the same reasoning all duties of subjection may be rejected. But as for this particular, let such proud #footnote[Esth 1:16, #emph[etc.]] dames note the outcome of Vashti’s stubbornness. As many excuses might be made for her, I think, as for anyone. For first, she was #footnote[#emph[Hebraei docent Vasthiam natam fuisse ex Baltasare rege Chaldaeorum. Feuard. in Esth.] 1. #emph[Alij, ex Cyro. Merl. in Esth.] \[The Hebrews teach that Vashti was born of Belshazzar, king of the Chaldeans. Others, of Cyrus.\]] of royal descent, being the daughter of a king. Secondly, she was then among the honorable women of the kingdom. Thirdly, the king was drunk when he sent for her. Fourthly, he sent for her to show her beauty before crowds of men, which was not seemly. But all these were not enough to excuse her fault and free her from blame. First, though she was of royal parentage, yet she was a wife, and her husband sent for her. Secondly, being among the noble women of the kingdom, she should all the more have shown herself a pattern of subjection in this respect. Thirdly, though he was drunk, yet he remained a husband, and what he commanded was something she could have done without sin. Fourthly, if she thought the thing unseemly, she should first have used all the fair means she could to be spared; but if she could not prevail by any of them, then (since the thing was not absolutely unlawful and a sin) she should have yielded.

#emph[Objection.] Her fault was not that as a #emph[wife] she did not come to her #emph[husband,] but that as a #emph[subject] she did not come to her #emph[sovereign.]

#emph[Answer.] Her fault was in both; and in the judgment passed against her the former was urged most, namely that by her #footnote[Esth 1:17.] example all women might learn to “despise their husbands.”

2\. #emph[Objection.] Her deed is censured so only by heathen men, who had no understanding of God’s word.

#emph[Answer.] 1. Holy Scripture, by the separate circumstances it notes so distinctly, intimates that her rebellious deed was a notorious fault; and accordingly both #footnote[Pellican. Lauater. Gualt. Strigel. Cuper. Merlin. Feuard. Bellator. Rupert. Isiodor. Hisp. Raban. alijque Comment. in Esth.] judicious commentators and preachers charge her with sinful disobedience to her husband.

2\. Though they were heathen, yet by the very light of nature they showed what subjection is required of wives to their husbands; and this makes the sin all the worse.

3\. Abimelech was only a heathen man; yet his sentence concerning a woman’s subjection, in these words, “he is to you a #footnote[Gen 20:16.] covering of the eyes,” is taken to be judicious, and, since it is approved by the Holy Spirit, to be a good proof. As for that particular case of Vashti, why is it recorded at such length in Scripture, if not for the instruction and admonition of wives?

=== §. 46. #emph[Of a wife’s readiness to do what her husband requires.]#footnote(numbering: (..) => "*")[Treatise 4, §. 29.]

A third particular instance of a wife’s readiness to yield to her husband’s command is #emph[to do whatever business he requires of her.] When three men suddenly came to Abram, and he wanted to receive them, he told his wife, “Quick! Prepare three measures of fine flour,” etc.#footnote[Gen 18:6.] And she did it accordingly. Jeroboam, having a weighty occasion to send to Ahijah the prophet, thought it fitting to send by his #footnote[1 Kgs 14:2, #emph[etc.]] wife; and she accordingly went (though a queen). She did as her husband wanted.

Contrary to this is the disposition of many wives, who will not do #footnote[Contrary: stubbornness in refusing to do a thing on command.] anything on command. If such a wife’s husband, wanting to receive a friend on short notice, uses Abram’s phrase, “Quick! Prepare,” etc., she will say, “Let him come and do it himself, if he wants it done so quickly; I will not be his drudge.” Or if, having a matter of importance and secrecy, he wants his wife to do it herself, she will reply, “I am none of your servants. Can you not give it to one of them, or do it yourself?” Yet such wives will be ready to command their husbands to do every trifle, and if he does not do it, they can reply, “Is this such a great matter? And may a wife not speak to her husband?” If the point of obedience were well learned, it would cast such wives in another mold.

These few particulars may serve for direction in many hundreds. I proceed to the other part of a wife’s active obedience, which concerns her husband’s reproofs.

=== §. 47. #emph[Of a wife’s meekly taking a reproof.]#footnote(numbering: (..) => "*")[Treatise 4, §. 34, 35.]

The husband, having authority over his wife, has power by virtue of it (indeed, it is his duty) to rebuke her when there is needful cause. It therefore follows by just consequence #footnote[A wife’s active obedience to her husband’s reproof. The best test of obedience.] that it is her duty to yield obedience to it. This ought to be done all the more because the chief test of sound obedience lies here. For nothing is so hard to stomach as reproof: she who yields when she is rebuked will yield much more when she is entreated. This point of obedience is shown in two ways.

1\. By meekness in taking a reproof.

2\. By striving to set right what is justly reproved. The very point of obedience lies especially in this latter; the former is a good preparation for it, without which it will hardly be done, at least not done well.

Meekness in this case is one of the chief fruits of #footnote[1\. She must meekly take reproof. 1 Pet 3:4. Gen 30:1.] that “meek and quiet spirit” which Saint Peter commends to wives. However justly Rachel deserved blame for coming in a fuming temper and with an imperious command to her husband, yet because she meekly took his sharp reproof (for she did not answer back against it, but meekly gave a direction for better accomplishing her desire), her example is commendable. Commendable, I say, not in the matter of her direction, but in her patient bearing of reproof.

Much wisdom may be learned from this. For when #footnote[Wisdom learned.] any meekly take a reproof, by this they hold down their anger and keep it from rising like a cloud before their understanding and darkening it; and so they can better judge whether the matter reproved is just or not, and whether it needs setting right or not. Those who are impatient of reproof, and fret and fume against it, cannot judge this so well. The virgin Mary made good use of Christ’s reproving her,#footnote[John 2:4, 5.] and by it learned and taught a good point of wisdom: namely, so to refer our affairs to Christ that we wait for his pleasure, and do not prescribe to him time, means, manner or any other such circumstances.

#emph[Question.] What if the husband’s reproof is bitter? #footnote[#emph[Suadeo mulieribus vt viros etiam asperiores perferant. Chrys. hom.] 26. #emph[in] 1 Cor 11. \[I urge wives to bear with even harsher husbands.\]]

#emph[Answer.] He forgets his place in this, yet she must not forget her duty because of it. If Jacob’s reproof is well noted, we will find it very sharp. For it is expressly said that “Jacob became angry with Rachel” (Gen 30:2), so that he spoke in anger. The manner and form of his words, being a question, and the #footnote[Excandescenti marito cede, & molli oratione iuva. Greg. Naz. ad Olymp. \[Give way to a husband who flares up, and help him with gentle words.\]] matter too, “Am I in the place of God,” etc., show #emph[sharpness.] Yet (as was shown before) she showed #emph[meekness.]

#emph[Question.] What if his reproof is unjust?

2\. #emph[Answer.] Even so, #emph[meekness] must not be forgotten. In such a case a wife may make a just defense to clear her own innocence and show her husband’s error. But if he refuses to hear her, or will not believe her, then (as Saint Peter speaks in another #footnote[1 Pet 2:19, 20.] case) she must, being “conscious of God,” “endure the pain of unjust suffering.”

The two reasons he gives there in that other case may fittingly be applied to this one.

1\. In general, this is “to be commended”: it is a grace, a glory to #footnote[χάρις.] her, a matter that deserves praise and commendation.

2\. In particular, it is “commendable before God”: however roughly and perversely their husbands #footnote[χάρις παρὰ Θεῷ.] may deal with them, yet God will graciously regard them, if in obedience to his ordinance they patiently bear their husbands’ unjust reproofs.

3\. I may add this reason too: that in this way they will show themselves good Christians indeed, in that they are not “overcome #footnote[Rom 12:21.] by evil.”

Contrary to this is the mind of those who will by no means tolerate a rebuke from their husbands. It makes no difference whether it is just or unjust: if their husbands reprove them, they are sure to have the reproof thrown back in their faces, and that with greater violence than it ever came from them. There are some who seem to be very good wives until they are tried by the touchstone of reproof. But then, though the reproof is most just in matter, most mild in manner, and given in private between their husbands and themselves, yet they grow so impatient, or rather mad, that they do not refrain from giving their husbands the most scornful speeches they can invent, using bitter curses and execrations besides, and threatening to drown or hang themselves if they are crossed in their wills. Indeed, further, if wise husbands bear with them in their anger, and after it has subsided tell them how unbecomingly they behaved for their place, they will seek to justify themselves and lay all the blame on their husbands for crossing them in their will. Or if they cannot help seeing their fault, yet they will only say, “It is my infirmity,” and yet they always continue in that infirmity; and though they make a show of fearing God, yet they do not labor to purge this corrupt disposition out of their hearts. This for the most part is where contentions between man and wife come from. If wives would learn to be subject in this point, many quarrels that arise between them from time to time would be calmed, if not prevented. Michal #footnote[2 Sam 6:20, #emph[etc.] Job 2:10.] the wife of David, and Job’s wife (though they gave just occasion to be most sharply reproved, yet) will rise up in judgment against these wives, because they were silent after they were reproved, and did not answer back. Solomon often calls those who cannot bear rebuke “scorners,”#footnote[Prov 9:7, 8.] so that by this wives show that they are very scornful.

=== §. 48. #emph[Of a wife’s readiness to set right what her husband justly reproves in her.]#footnote(numbering: (..) => "*")[Treatise 4, §. 35.]

A further degree of obedience in bearing reproof is that #footnote[2\. She must set right what is justly reproved.] #emph[a wife should readily set right what is justly reproved by her husband.] I say #emph[justly,] because where there is no fault, no amendment is needed. Patience may be needed (as was shown before), but no repentance for what is not amiss. But where anything is amiss, it must be set right. Rachel did amiss in #footnote[Gen 31:19.] bringing idols into her husband’s house. Her husband, in #footnote[Gen 35:2, 4.] telling her among others to “get rid of the foreign gods,” reproved them all. On this she, with all the rest, “gave Jacob all their foreign gods.” This was a good amendment.

A reproof may justly be given either for a good duty omitted or for an evil thing committed, and the setting right, or amendment, must answer to it. A duty formerly omitted must, after the reproof, be more carefully observed and performed, if it is a continual duty and can be performed again; otherwise the amendment is a testimony of true sorrow for that fault. When an evil has been committed, if any means can be used to make up the hurt and repair the injury that followed from it, it must be done; if not, sorrow must be shown, as before, and care taken that the same, or the like, is not committed again.

As a good conscience requires as much of all Christians, whoever reproves them, so the respect a wife owes to a husband requires it in a special manner. Otherwise her fault is doubled: 1. by continuing in her sin; 2\. by disobedience to her husband.

Contrary to this is the spirit of those who grow worse for reproof, #footnote[Contrary: to grow worse for being reproved. Prov 9:8.] being like those “scorners” (of whom Solomon speaks) who “hate” those who reprove them. It is the saying of some wives that if their husbands would let them alone they would do better, but that on rebuke they will never amend: the more their husbands find fault, the more they will go on doing what they do. What other judgment can be given of such than the one the wise man gives, that there is “more hope #footnote[Prov 26:12.] for a fool” than for them? So much for submission.

=== §. 49. #emph[Of a wife’s contentment with her husband’s present condition.]#footnote(numbering: (..) => "*")[Treatise 4, §. 50.]

#emph[Contentment] is also a part of #emph[obedience.] It concerns a man’s outward condition and means, in and with which a wife must rest satisfied and content, whether it is high or low, great or lowly, wealthy or needy, above, equal to, or below the condition she was in before marriage. Indeed, though a man has once been great in wealth, yet if he declines in it and is brought to a lowly condition, she ought to rest content. Job implies as much in his reply to his wife, saying, “Should we receive #footnote[Job 2:10 expounded.] good from the hand of God, and not receive evil?” The evil he speaks of was the loss of his goods, servants and children, together with other miseries that Satan, by God’s permission, inflicted on him. The #emph[receiving of evil] he speaks of was a resting content with it and a patient bearing of it. Evil may be laid on anyone, and so they may be forced to bear it; but only those who are content with it receive it. Now since he uses the plural number (WE), and, speaking to his wife, says #emph[(should we not receive evil),] he shows that his wife ought, as well as he, to rest content in that poor and miserable condition. For

1\. Man and wife being #emph[one flesh] by virtue of their marriage union, both his advancement and his abasement are #emph[hers:] as she rises with him, so she falls with him. Therefore, as she is willing to be advanced with him, so she must be content to be brought low with him.

2\. If at the time of marriage her husband was of lower condition than she, she voluntarily put herself into that lowly condition. For a wife takes her husband (as he takes her) #emph[for better, for worse, for richer, for poorer.] And will she not be content with her own act? If after marriage his condition declines and grows lower than it was, she is to be persuaded that God ordered it so, and that God aimed at #emph[her] humbling as well as #emph[his.] On that ground she ought, in dutiful submission to God’s overruling providence, to be patient and content. Job implies this in the phrase #emph[should we not receive evil from the hand of God?] and in this one, “the #smallcaps[Lord] has taken away.”

3\. A wife’s contentment is a great ease to her husband when he lies under a cross, and it makes the burden seem much lighter than it otherwise would, at least if he is a kind husband and moved by his wife’s distress, as he ought to be. For a loving husband in every distress is more troubled for his wife than for himself.

=== §. 50. #emph[Of wives’ discontent at their husbands’ condition.]

Contrary to this is the impatience and discontent of wives at #footnote[Discontent shown.] the lowliness and (as they think) the inferiority of their husbands’ condition, which is shown in many ways.

1\. Some, when they are married, finding their husbands’ means #footnote[1\. By repenting of their marriage.] weaker than they imagined, repent of their marriage, and do not hesitate to tell their husbands that if they had known before that they were no better men than they find them to be, they would never have taken them for husbands. In this, first, they betray their foolish indiscretion by saying, when it is too late, #emph[if I had] #footnote[#emph[Non est sapientis dicere non putâram. Cic. Offic. lib.] 1. \[It is not the part of a wise man to say, “I had not thought it.”\]] #emph[thought this;] and besides, they show their own rashness and thoughtlessness, in that they gave their hands and pledged their faith to those whom they did not know. If they say they were deceived by their relatives, whom they trusted, I answer that marriage is too weighty a matter to be left wholly to the trust of relatives. Everyone who agrees to be married ought to know well the person to whom in this case they yield; and above all they ought to seek direction, help and blessing from God. If, in spite of all the means they could possibly use, they are deceived, they are to look to God and see his providence in it, and to weigh duly whether the Lord has crossed their desire for their humbling, or to test their patience, wisdom and other such graces, or else to wean them from some vain and worldly delights to which they were too much addicted.

2\. Others, seeing that their husbands’ means are decayed #footnote[2\. By impatient behavior.] and wasted, never inquire into the cause, but lay all the blame on their husbands, and with their discontented looks, angry words and impatient behavior so vex their hearts that they make the cross much heavier than it otherwise could be. Even if the estate should be ruined by a husband’s wastefulness, a wife ought still to look to God’s providence in it, as was noted before.

3\. Others, scorning to stoop and come down to their #footnote[3\. By refusing to stoop.] husbands’ present condition, through their pride and vainglory are a great means of making his condition much worse. For as long as they can get it by any means, they will not cut back anything of their fine clothing, delicate food, rich furnishings and other such things, which cause their husbands great expense. By this it also often happens that husbands are thought to be wealthier than they really are, and so greater taxes and charges than they can bear are laid on them for king, country, church, poor and the like.

4\. Others, through discontent, lie lazing long in their beds, #footnote[4\. By refusing to help.] or sit idle when they are up, and will not take the least bit more pains to raise up and increase their husbands’ means. By this God is provoked more and more to weaken their means, so that he may punish such pride and laziness in wives all the more.
